  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nPrimary forecast focus and challenge remains heavy rain timing \nand location through Sunday.\n\nA large area of convection currently extends from far northeast\nIA across most of southern WI and is slowly drifting south into\nfar northern IL. This activity will continue to slowly move \ninto northern IL during the predawn hours and into the I-80\ncorridor by mid/late morning. There remains some uncertainty \nfor where the heaviest rains will setup and how long they'll \npersist in any given area. The heaviest rains this morning may \nend up falling along and northeast of a Rockford to Chicago \nline. Scattered showers and possibly a thunderstorm may continue\nthis afternoon. Current flood watch area has this threat \nhandled well both for area and timing, ending at 18z today and \nno changes are planned.\n\nAnother period of thunderstorms with heavy rain is expected\nlater this evening, through the overnight hours and into Sunday\nmorning, likely slowly sagging south across the cwa during this\ntime period, which may bring the heaviest and most widespread \nrains to at least parts of the area. Where these may overlap \nwith heavy rains from this morning is still uncertain. Another \nflood watch is likely from late this evening into Sunday \nmorning, for the same areas as the current watch and possibly \nextending another row of counties south to the Illinois and \nKankakee River Valleys. Given the current watch end time and a \nlarge time gap until the next wave tonight, will hold off on a \nnew flood watch for tonight.\n\nThe new SPC Day1 and Day2 outlooks have trimmed the level 1\nsevere risk to the far southern portions of the local area.\nThere will still be the potential for a few stronger storms and\nperhaps an isolated severe storm or two, mainly across the\nsouthern cwa.\n\nA few of the models, NAM/HRRR, suggest another period of heavy\nrain during the day on Sunday. Confidence is low during this\nperiod, but if these trends were to continue, a second possible \nflood watch may need to be extended out into this time period as\nwell.\n\nHigh waves and dangerous swimming conditions will continue into\nthis afternoon at IL Lake Michigan beaches as easterly winds\nturn more southeasterly. Northeast winds are expected to quickly\nincrease early Sunday morning and then remain fairly strong\nthrough the middle of next week, resulting in several days of\nhigh waves and dangerous swimming conditions at IL and IN Lake\nMichigan beaches. An additional beach hazard statement will be\nneeded for this long time period.\n\nThe models are now trending toward an upper level low across the\nregion through midweek. While below normal temperatures still\nlook on track, there will likely be at least low chances for\nshowers, especially during the afternoon/evening as various weak\nwaves rotate around the upper low. cms",
